Throughout 2026, I combined hands-on engineering, solution design, production support, and team coordination to advance the Business Banking payroll platform. My contributions focused on reliable payment processing, automated recovery and reconciliation, and effective integration across Gusto, CIB, PIM, and downstream payment services. I also managed the team during my manager’s August vacation, maintaining continuity alongside my individual delivery responsibilities.

1. Designed and implemented payroll Store and Forward recovery.

I designed and implemented the Store and Forward framework to support controlled retries and recovery from processing failures. This included retry-outbox management, retry tracking, expiration handling, and strategies to restart a processing journey or resume from a specific step. I addressed scenarios involving newer events arriving while earlier retries remained pending, considering ordering, idempotency, and stale-event handling. This established a reusable foundation for payroll recovery and operational support.

2. Delivered PIM V2 integration and strengthened payment-lifecycle processing.

I integrated PIM V2 capabilities for payroll submission, cancellation, and instruction listing/retrieval. I helped restructure processing into clearer stages for identifier generation, request submission, instruction retrieval, and posting. I worked through retry and cancellation behavior across parent and child processing flows, making a complex downstream integration more maintainable and easier to investigate.

3. Improved reconciliation and automated batch processing.

I restructured reconciliation monitoring and related payment, payroll, and future-payroll jobs using Spring Batch. I contributed to moving reconciliation from end-of-day processing toward hourly monitoring, using updated timestamps and the last successful run to define incremental processing windows. I also worked on contractor-payment group linking to preserve the relationship between payment groups and individual payments. These improvements supported more timely identification of discrepancies and more systematic recovery.

4. Accelerated delivery through practical use of AI-assisted engineering.

I used Copilot, including its agentic interface, and LLM Suite to support repository understanding, code analysis, and implementation. I learned to create Markdown instruction files that provide project context, implementation guidance, and task-specific requirements.

A concrete example was the hire-date backfill job: with support from Copilot’s agentic interface, I completed the implementation overnight and took it through testing and production deployment within one day. The solution included configurable batching, scheduled execution, and a mitigation controller for targeted processing of selected source payee IDs. This demonstrated how well-defined context and AI assistance could support rapid delivery of a complete operational capability.

5. Supported production resiliency and clarified critical payroll behavior.

I supported pilot/PVT activities, downstream testing, production deployments, and the West-to-East resiliency event. I investigated critical issues involving source-data validation, PIM timeouts and idempotency, asynchronous status ordering, and deployment/configuration dependencies.

I also led discussions with Product and partner teams on auto-cancellation and retry behavior. I translated definitive failures, uncertain downstream outcomes, duplicate responses, cutoff scenarios, and retry exhaustion into concrete business cases to support alignment on expected payroll handling.

